######
#
# Build and Install ATS2 (ATS/Postiats)
#
######
#
# Author: Hongwei Xi
# Authoremail: gmhwxi AT gmail DOT com
# Start time: 2013-09-02
#
# Last update: 2026-10-21 (the build of ATS2 is self-hosting; ATS1 is not
# needed for anything).
#
######
#
# There are two ways to get ATS2:
#
#   A. the release tarball (for the users of ATS2)
#   B. the git repository (for the developers of ATS2)
#
######

######
#
# Step 0: the pre-requisites
#
######

* a C compiler (gcc or clang)
* GNU make
* python3 (it generates the arena encodings of the compiler's syntax trees)
* libgmp (the package libgmp-dev on e.g. Debian and Ubuntu)

libgmp is needed by the default build (C3NSTRINTKND=gmpknd). It can be
avoided by building the compiler with the integer backend instead:

  make -f Makefile_devl C3NSTRINTKND=intknd all

######
#
# Step A: the release tarball
#
######

The current release of ATS2 can be downloaded from

https://sourceforge.net/projects/ats2-lang/

There are three tarballs:

  ATS2-Postiats-x.y.z.tgz          the compiler, the libraries and the tools
  ATS2-Postiats-contrib-x.y.z.tgz  the packages of the external contrib
                                   repository (ATS-Postiats-contrib)
  ATS2-Postiats-include-x.y.z.tgz  the headers and the CATS files that are
                                   needed to use ATS2 from C

Say the tarball you download is named ATS2-Postiats-x.y.z.tgz. Untar it:

  tar -vxfz ATS2-Postiats-x.y.z.tgz

which creates a directory named ATS-Postiats.

The tarball ships the bootstrap C bundle (src/CBOOT), so it can be built
without any ATS2 compiler: no ATS1 is needed.

  cd ATS-Postiats
  ./configure --prefix=/path/to/MYATS
  make
  make install

The install step puts the libraries in

  /path/to/MYATS/lib/ats2-postiats-x.y.z

which is the PATSHOME of the installation:

  export PATSHOME=/path/to/MYATS/lib/ats2-postiats-x.y.z
  export PATH=${PATH}:${PATSHOME}/bin

Then `patscc` (the ATS2 compiler driver) and `patsopt` (the ATS2 compiler)
are on your path.

######
#
# Step B: the git repository
#
######

  git clone https://github.com/githwxi/ATS-Postiats.git
  cd ATS-Postiats

The compiler (src/) is written in ATS2 and compiles itself, so a first
patsopt (the stage-0 compiler) is needed. It is one of:

* any ATS2 compiler (an installed release or an earlier build of this
  repository);
* the bootstrap C bundle of this repository: `make -C src CBOOT`
  generates it (it needs a working patsopt, see src/CBOOT/README) and
  `make -C src/CBOOT` builds it (it needs only a C compiler and libgmp);
  the resulting compiler is src/CBOOT/patsopt.

Then the compiler and the tools are built with

  make -f Makefile_devl PATSOPT=/path/to/stage0/patsopt all

For an installed ATS2 as the stage-0 compiler, its own prelude is needed
as well:

  make -f Makefile_devl PATSOPT=/usr/lib/ats2-postiats-0.4.2/bin/patsopt \
    STAGE0HOME=/usr/lib/ats2-postiats-0.4.2 all

Without the argument PATSOPT, the stage-0 compiler is $PATSHOME/bin/patsopt
if it exists, else the patsopt of your PATH.

The build produces bin/patsopt, bin/patscc, bin/myatscc, the library
src/libatsopt.a and the runtime library ccomp/atslib/lib/libatslib.a.
The tree itself is the PATSHOME of the build:

  export PATSHOME=$(pwd)
  export PATH=${PATH}:${PATSHOME}/bin

The other tools of the repository are built with:

  make -f Makefile_devl bin_atsdoc      # the .atxt text processor
  make -f Makefile_devl bin_pats2xhtml   # the syntax highlighter

######
#
# Step C: the tests
#
######

There is no unit-test framework: the tests are regression suites.

  make -C src check                # typecheck every module of the compiler
  make -C src test                 # the compiler's own suites (needs bin/patscc)
  make -C utils/atsdoc test        # atsdoc and libatsdoc
  make -C utils/libatsynmark test  # libatsynmark and pats2xhtml
  make -C doc -f Makefile_test testall  # the examples and the book code

######
#
# Step D: the release tarballs
#
######

The release tarballs are built from the repository by

  make -f Makefile_devl bin_atsdoc    # atsdoc generates the packaging scripts
  make -C src CBOOT                    # the bootstrap C bundle
  make -C doc/DISTRIB atspackaging     # fills in doc/DISTRIB/ATS-Postiats
  make -C doc/DISTRIB atspacktarzvcf_gmp   # ATS2-Postiats-x.y.z.tgz
  make -C doc/DISTRIB -f Makefile_inclats tarzvcf  # ATS2-Postiats-include-x.y.z.tgz

(use atspacktarzvcf_int instead of atspacktarzvcf_gmp for the variant that
does not depend on libgmp).

The script share/SCRIPT/build_release.sh does all of this for a given
version; it checks that the version of the release is declared consistently
(share/SCRIPT/check_release_version.sh).

######
#
# Step E: the nix build
#
######

  nix-shell nix/shell.nix   # a shell with ATS2 built from this tree
  nix build                 # the same, with the flake

###### end of [INSTALL] ######
